        "problem": "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $P(0,-2,-4)$ and $Q(5,0,1).$",
        "worked_solution": "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=p+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $P(0,-2,-4)$ and $Q(5,0,1)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\u27e85,0,1\u27e9-\u27e80,-2,-4\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e85,2,5\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
